WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.388
Add an invented nonsense string to a

00:00:03.408 --> 00:00:04.663
genuine question.

00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:07.215
If every word is required and the invented

00:00:07.235 --> 00:00:10.179
string occurs nowhere, what should happen?

00:00:10.341 --> 00:00:12.744
Under that strict AND assumption, the

00:00:12.764 --> 00:00:14.625
result set should be empty.

00:00:17.027 --> 00:00:18.866
In the chapter's scite screenshot,

00:00:19.003 --> 00:00:21.135
useful-looking results remain and the

00:00:21.155 --> 00:00:23.235
interface reports millions.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:25.735
That challenges the strict-AND assumption.

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:28.326
It does not prove the system stopped

00:00:28.346 --> 00:00:31.058
matching words, or identify a semantic

00:00:31.078 --> 00:00:32.500
retrieval architecture.

00:00:32.663 --> 00:00:35.117
Our on-screen nonsense token is a teaching

00:00:35.137 --> 00:00:36.160
illustration.

00:00:37.091 --> 00:00:38.952
The supplied Google Scholar example

00:00:38.972 --> 00:00:41.323
reports about nine point four million

00:00:41.343 --> 00:00:43.500
results for open access.

00:00:43.649 --> 00:00:45.885
Google's help documentation limits the

00:00:45.905 --> 00:00:48.784
viewable results for one query to a

00:00:48.804 --> 00:00:49.634
thousand.

00:00:50.185 --> 00:00:52.816
This diagram contains nine thousand four

00:00:52.836 --> 00:00:54.143
hundred equal marks.

00:00:54.318 --> 00:00:56.321
Each represents a thousand reported

00:00:56.341 --> 00:00:59.787
matches. One highlighted mark represents

00:00:59.846 --> 00:01:01.668
the documented display cap.

00:01:01.831 --> 00:01:04.285
The reported count does not establish that

00:01:04.305 --> 00:01:06.347
every matching record was individually

00:01:06.367 --> 00:01:07.789
scored or ranked.

00:01:07.978 --> 00:01:10.186
How was the visible fraction chosen?

00:01:11.113 --> 00:01:13.278
In the author's August twenty twenty-six

00:01:13.298 --> 00:01:15.404
Semantic Scholar observation, the

00:01:15.424 --> 00:01:17.376
natural-language question returned

00:01:17.396 --> 00:01:18.780
thirteen results.

00:01:18.955 --> 00:01:21.654
The shorter, unquoted keyword query

00:01:21.674 --> 00:01:23.845
returned about thirty-five thousand three

00:01:23.865 --> 00:01:26.023
hundred. The bars use a labeled

00:01:26.043 --> 00:01:28.265
logarithmic scale; they are not

00:01:28.285 --> 00:01:29.798
proportional counts.

00:01:30.357 --> 00:01:32.580
The author also judged the question's

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.428
leading results less relevant, using the

00:01:35.448 --> 00:01:38.275
known literature and inspected results.

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:40.582
That judgement does not follow from the

00:01:40.602 --> 00:01:43.598
counts alone. The comparison establishes

00:01:43.618 --> 00:01:44.911
visible behavior.

00:01:45.087 --> 00:01:47.129
The product's name does not establish its

00:01:47.149 --> 00:01:48.210
architecture.

00:01:49.122 --> 00:01:50.967
The first puzzle leaves an admission rule

00:01:50.987 --> 00:01:53.428
open. The second leaves ranking and

00:01:53.448 --> 00:01:55.244
display selection open.

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:57.423
The third leaves query interpretation

00:01:57.443 --> 00:02:00.889
open. Screenshots show outputs; they do

00:02:00.909 --> 00:02:02.938
not expose a complete pipeline.

00:02:03.516 --> 00:02:05.964
The chapter revisits the first two through

00:02:05.984 --> 00:02:08.631
lexical retrieval in Chapter Four, and the

00:02:08.651 --> 00:02:11.143
third through mechanisms in Part Two,

00:02:11.383 --> 00:02:13.089
especially Chapter Seven.

00:02:13.265 --> 00:02:15.165
Keep the questions open while learning

00:02:15.185 --> 00:02:16.504
what could explain them.

00:02:16.667 --> 00:02:18.283
These observations were taken from the

00:02:18.303 --> 00:02:20.576
chapter, not rerun here.

00:02:21.478 --> 00:02:23.697
The chapter separates output format from

00:02:23.717 --> 00:02:25.165
retrieval iteration.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:27.936
Across: ranked records or a written

00:02:27.956 --> 00:02:31.930
answer. Upward: a single or limited pass,

00:02:31.951 --> 00:02:33.387
or repeated searches.

00:02:33.575 --> 00:02:36.119
That gives quick search, quick answer,

00:02:36.307 --> 00:02:38.709
iterative search and deep research.

00:02:39.268 --> 00:02:42.127
Neither axis records agency: who chooses

00:02:42.147 --> 00:02:43.248
the next action?

00:02:43.397 --> 00:02:46.109
A fixed workflow and an agent can occupy

00:02:46.129 --> 00:02:49.215
the same box. Repeated retrieval and

00:02:49.235 --> 00:02:51.238
model-chosen actions are different

00:02:51.258 --> 00:02:54.228
properties. This diagram maps concepts

00:02:54.248 --> 00:02:56.315
rather than assigning products to current

00:02:56.335 --> 00:02:57.346
categories.

00:02:58.246 --> 00:03:00.837
A language model can write a query, invent

00:03:00.857 --> 00:03:03.053
search text such as a pseudo-document,

00:03:03.319 --> 00:03:05.799
reorder candidates, or control the next

00:03:05.819 --> 00:03:06.919
retrieval action.

00:03:07.094 --> 00:03:09.368
Those are uses inside retrieval.

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:11.507
It can also write the final answer.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:15.063
So a vendor saying uses an LLM has located

00:03:15.083 --> 00:03:18.053
no component. Ask which decision it makes,

00:03:18.073 --> 00:03:20.566
which evidence it receives and what can be

00:03:20.586 --> 00:03:23.659
inspected. A polished report alone cannot

00:03:23.679 --> 00:03:25.037
answer those questions.

00:03:25.938 --> 00:03:28.306
Return to the missing landmark paper.

00:03:28.481 --> 00:03:30.665
Ask whether it was searchable, whether it

00:03:30.685 --> 00:03:32.972
became a candidate, whether it reached the

00:03:32.992 --> 00:03:35.394
working set, and whether the writer cited

00:03:35.414 --> 00:03:38.332
it. Each answer needs different evidence.

00:03:38.884 --> 00:03:40.913
Then ask whether the selected papers help

00:03:40.933 --> 00:03:42.819
with the actual information need.

00:03:42.982 --> 00:03:45.190
Chapter One has given us the questions.

00:03:45.353 --> 00:03:47.395
Chapter Two starts with the strictest

00:03:47.415 --> 00:03:49.650
possible admission rule: words that a

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:51.440
record must contain.
